4307fe3b-95d4-4c04-ada3-e63f28a41441	Valsartan	Angiotensin II Receptor Blockers (ARBs)	Cardiovascular System	Diovan	ARB.	Hypertension, heart failure, post-MI.	80-320 mg daily.\n\nDose adjustments: No renal adjustment needed for mild-moderate.	Pregnancy.	Dizziness, hypotension.	K+ supplements, lithium.	Contraindicated.	BP, K+, Cr.	Valsartan is absorbed orally with an absolute bioavailability of about 23 to 25 percent, and peak plasma concentrations occur around 2 to 4 hours after dosing. Food reduces peak concentration and AUC by roughly 40 to 50 percent, though the clinical effect on antihypertensive efficacy is minor, so it can be taken with or without food as long as this is done consistently. Valsartan is highly protein bound, about 94 to 97 percent, mainly to albumin, and undergoes minimal hepatic metabolism, with less than 20 percent converted to an inactive hydroxy metabolite via limited CYP2C9 involvement, meaning it is largely unaffected by most CYP450 interactions. It is primarily a substrate of the OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 hepatic uptake transporters, which is relevant to certain drug interactions. Elimination is predominantly biliary and fecal, accounting for about 83 percent of the dose, with roughly 13 percent excreted renally as unchanged drug. The terminal elimination half-life is approximately 6 hours. Steady state is reached within about 1 week of once daily dosing, and there is no significant accumulation with chronic use. No dose adjustment is required for mild to moderate renal impairment, but caution is advised in severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance under 10 mL per minute) due to limited data. In hepatic impairment, mild to moderate disease without cholestasis requires no dose adjustment, but valsartan is contraindicated or should be used with great caution in biliary cirrhosis or biliary obstruction because elimination is markedly reduced, and it has not been studied in severe hepatic impairment.	Avoid combining with ACE inhibitors or aliskiren, especially in diabetics or renal impairment, due to risk of hyperkalemia, hypotension, and acute kidney injury. Check potassium and renal function within 1 to 2 weeks of starting or up titrating, and periodically thereafter. Contraindicated in pregnancy, particularly the second and third trimesters, due to fetal renal toxicity and death; stop as soon as pregnancy is confirmed. Not recommended in breastfeeding given limited data. Counsel patients about first dose hypotension, especially if volume depleted or on diuretics. Unlike ACE inhibitors, cough and angioedema are uncommon, making valsartan a good substitute for ACE inhibitor intolerant patients.	f	2026-08-12 20:31:45.18589+05	2026-08-17 20:22:49.871131+05
