dfd64973-222d-47c1-bbc7-4f9bb8244392	Famotidine	Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs)	Gastrointestinal System	Pepcid	H2 receptor antagonist.	GERD, PUD, Heartburn.	20-40 mg daily or BID.\n\nDose adjustments: Renal: reduce dose if CrCl <50.	Hypersensitivity.	Headache, dizziness, constipation.	Drugs requiring acidic pH (ketoconazole).	Safe.	Renal function.	Famotidine is worth flagging up front: it is an H2 receptor antagonist, not a proton pump inhibitor, despite the category label given here. Oral bioavailability is roughly 40 to 45 percent and is not meaningfully affected by food, though antacids can reduce absorption slightly if dosed simultaneously. Peak plasma concentration is reached in about 1 to 3 hours after an oral dose. Protein binding is low, around 15 to 20 percent, and the drug distributes well into tissue including breast milk. Metabolism is minimal and hepatic, via sulfoxidation, and famotidine does not undergo significant CYP450 mediated metabolism, which is why it has a much cleaner interaction profile than cimetidine. Elimination is predominantly renal, with 65 to 70 percent of an intravenous dose and about 25 to 30 percent of an oral dose excreted unchanged in urine via both glomerular filtration and tubular secretion. Elimination half-life is approximately 2.5 to 3.5 hours in patients with normal renal function, but this is prolonged substantially in renal impairment, reaching 20 hours or more in patients with a creatinine clearance below 10 mL per minute, so dose or frequency reduction is required as renal function declines. Hepatic impairment does not significantly alter kinetics given the minimal hepatic metabolism.	Correct the category: famotidine is an H2 blocker, not a PPI, and is often chosen over PPIs for short term or on demand reflux control, nocturnal acid breakthrough, or when a cleaner CYP profile is needed. Reduce dose in renal impairment to avoid accumulation, which has been linked to confusion, especially in elderly or critically ill patients. Tachyphylaxis can develop with continuous H2 blocker use over days to weeks, unlike PPIs. Unlike cimetidine, it does not inhibit CYP450 enzymes, so drug interactions are minimal. Useful adjunct with PPIs for refractory nocturnal symptoms, but not a substitute for erosive esophagitis healing.	f	2026-08-12 20:31:45.18589+05	2026-08-17 18:51:15.405993+05
