8452aa3f-c155-4c40-a712-2effdad1ad2f	Lisinopril	ACE Inhibitors	Cardiovascular System	Zestril, Prinivil	Inhibits Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme, reducing Angiotensin II levels.	Hypertension, heart failure, post-MI.	10-40 mg once daily.\n\nDose adjustments: Renal impairment: adjust based on CrCl.	History of angioedema, pregnancy, bilateral renal artery stenosis.	Dry cough, dizziness, hyperkalemia, hypotension.	Potassium supplements, NSAIDs, lithium, diuretics.	Contraindicated in pregnancy (fetal toxicity).	BP, serum potassium, renal function (Cr, BUN).	Absorption: ~25% (range 6-60%), not affected by food; unlike other ACE inhibitors, lisinopril is not a prodrug and does not require hepatic activation. Onset of action ~1 hour, peak effect 6-8 hours, duration ~24 hours. Distribution: minimal plasma protein binding. Metabolism: not metabolized by the liver; excreted unchanged. Half-life: ~12 hours (effective accumulation half-life), but terminal half-life may extend to 30+ hours due to slow dissociation from tissue ACE binding sites. Elimination: renally excreted unchanged in urine; dose adjustment required in renal impairment (CrCl <30 mL/min); not significantly removed by peritoneal dialysis but is removed by hemodialysis.	Monitor renal function and potassium at baseline and after initiation/dose changes; risk of hyperkalemia, especially with renal impairment, potassium-sparing diuretics, or NSAIDs. Contraindicated in pregnancy (fetotoxic) and with history of angioedema. Avoid combination with ARBs or aliskiren (renal risk, hyperkalemia). First-dose hypotension possible, especially if volume-depleted or on diuretics. Persistent dry cough is a common reason for discontinuation. Since not hepatically metabolized, useful in hepatic impairment but requires renal dose adjustment. Watch for rare but serious angioedema, which can occur even after long-term use.	f	2026-08-12 20:31:45.18589+05	2026-08-12 20:41:01.163499+05
7a4b739a-1d86-4973-b3e8-0a7afd6bcbb9	Semaglutide	Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) receptor agonist	Endocrine System / Metabolic Disorders (also used in Cardiovascular risk reduction and Weight Management)	USA: Ozempic (injectable, diabetes), Wegovy (injectable, weight management), Rybelsus (oral, diabetes)\nUK: Ozempic, Wegovy, Rybelsus\nPakistan: Ozempic\nIndia: Ozempic, Rybelsus\nEU (general): Ozempic, Wegovy, Rybelsus	Semaglutide is a GLP-1 receptor agonist with ~94% sequence homology to native human GLP-1. It binds to and activates the GLP-1 receptor on pancreatic beta cells, stimulating glucose-dependent insulin secretion and suppressing inappropriate glucagon secretion, thereby lowering blood glucose. It also slows gastric emptying and acts centrally on hypothalamic appetite regulation centers to reduce appetite and caloric intake, promoting weight loss. Structural modifications (fatty diacid side chain, albumin binding, and amino acid substitutions) confer resistance to DPP-4 degradation, resulting in a prolonged half-life suitable for once-weekly (injectable) or once-daily (oral) dosing.	1) Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus - as an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control (Ozempic, Rybelsus).\n2) Chronic weight management in adults with obesity (BMI ≥30) or overweight (BMI ≥27) with at least one weight-related comorbidity, as an adjunct to reduced-calorie diet and increased physical activity (Wegovy).\n3) Reduction of major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE) - cardiovascular death, non-fatal MI, non-fatal stroke - in adults with type 2 diabetes and established cardiovascular disease (Ozempic).\n4) Reduction of risk of major cardiovascular events in adults with established CVD and obesity/overweight (Wegovy - expanded indication).\n5) Off-label: PCOS-associated obesity/insulin resistance, NAFLD/NASH (investigational).	ADULT:\nOzempic (subcutaneous, T2DM/CV risk reduction): Start 0.25 mg once weekly x4 weeks (not for glycemic control, dose escalation only); increase to 0.5 mg once weekly; if additional glycemic control needed after ≥4 weeks, increase to 1 mg once weekly; may further increase to 2 mg once weekly (max) after ≥4 weeks at 1 mg dose.\n\nWegovy (subcutaneous, weight management): Start 0.25 mg once weekly x4 weeks, then titrate every 4 weeks: 0.5 mg → 1 mg → 1.7 mg → 2.4 mg (maintenance/target dose) once weekly. If not tolerated, may extend titration interval.\n\nRybelsus (oral, T2DM): Start 3 mg once daily x30 days (dose-initiation, not for glycemic efficacy); increase to 7 mg once daily; may increase to 14 mg once daily after ≥30 days if needed. Take on empty stomach with ≤4 oz plain water, ≥30 minutes before first food/beverage/other oral medications of the day.\n\nPEDIATRIC: Wegovy approved for weight management in adolescents ≥12 years with obesity (same titration schedule as adults, target 2.4 mg SC weekly). Ozempic/Rybelsus not established in pediatric T2DM (<18 years) as of current labeling in most markets (safety/efficacy not established for Ozempic <18 years in many regions; check local label updates).\n\nRenal/Hepatic impairment: No dose adjustment required; use with caution in severe GI disease.	Personal or family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC); Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia syndrome type 2 (MEN 2); History of serious hypersensitivity reaction to semaglutide or any component of the formulation; Pregnancy (discontinue at least 2 months prior to planned pregnancy).	Very common (>10%): Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, constipation.\nCommon (1-10%): Decreased appetite, dyspepsia, eructation, flatulence, gastroenteritis, gastroesophageal reflux disease, injection site reactions, fatigue, dizziness, headache, cholelithiasis (gallstones).\nSerious/Uncommon: Acute pancreatitis, acute kidney injury (often secondary to GI fluid loss/dehydration), diabetic retinopathy complications (worsening in patients with pre-existing retinopathy, especially with rapid glycemic improvement), hypoglycemia (especially with concurrent insulin/sulfonylurea), gallbladder disease (cholecystitis), hypersensitivity reactions (rare anaphylaxis/angioedema), increased heart rate.\nBoxed Warning: Risk of thyroid C-cell tumors (thyroid medullary carcinoma) - observed in rodent studies; human relevance unknown but contraindicated in high-risk patients.	Insulin and insulin secretagogues (sulfonylureas): Increased risk of hypoglycemia - dose reduction of secretagogue may be needed.\nOral medications requiring rapid GI absorption: Delayed gastric emptying may impact absorption of concomitant oral drugs (e.g., oral contraceptives - though studies show minimal clinical effect; monitor efficacy).\nWarfarin: Possible altered anticoagulant effect due to delayed gastric emptying - monitor INR when initiating.\nLevothyroxine and other oral medications with narrow therapeutic index taken with Rybelsus: administer as directed with appropriate spacing due to absorption enhancer (SNAC) effects.\nAlcohol: May increase risk of pancreatitis and hypoglycemia.	Pregnancy: Not recommended. Based on animal data showing embryo-fetal toxicity, potential risk to fetus. Discontinue semaglutide at least 2 months before a planned pregnancy due to long washout period (~5 half-lives). No adequate human data available.\nLactation: Unknown if excreted in human breast milk. Not recommended during breastfeeding due to lack of safety data; weigh risk/benefit of continuing or discontinuing.	Baseline and periodic HbA1c and fasting blood glucose.\nRenal function (BUN/creatinine), especially during dose escalation or in patients with GI adverse effects/dehydration.\nSigns/symptoms of pancreatitis (severe abdominal pain, nausea/vomiting).\nSigns/symptoms of gallbladder disease (cholelithiasis, cholecystitis).\nThyroid: Monitor for signs of thyroid tumors (neck mass, dysphagia, dyspnea, persistent hoarseness) - routine calcitonin/thyroid ultrasound not recommended unless clinically indicated.\nDiabetic retinopathy status (especially with pre-existing disease, at treatment initiation and periodically).\nWeight, BMI, waist circumference (for weight management indication).\nHeart rate.\nInjection site reactions (for SC formulations).	Absorption: SC injection - peak plasma concentration reached in 1-3 days; steady state achieved after 4-5 weeks of once-weekly dosing. Oral (Rybelsus) - absorbed in stomach facilitated by SNAC (sodium N-(8-[2-hydroxybenzoyl]amino)caprylate) absorption enhancer; low absolute bioavailability (~0.4-1%).\nDistribution: Extensive plasma protein binding (>99%) to albumin, contributing to prolonged half-life. Volume of distribution ~12.5 L.\nMetabolism: Metabolized via proteolytic cleavage of the peptide backbone and sequential beta-oxidation of the fatty acid side chain; no single organ is major route of elimination.\nElimination: Primarily via urine and feces as metabolites. Elimination half-life ~7 days (168 hours), allowing once-weekly SC dosing; supports once-daily oral dosing due to slow clearance.\nSpecial populations: No significant PK differences based on age, sex, race, ethnicity, body weight (mild effect), or renal/hepatic impairment (mild-moderate); not studied extensively in severe renal/hepatic impairment or ESRD.	• Titrate slowly to minimize GI adverse effects (most common reason for discontinuation); if severe GI intolerance occurs, consider delaying dose escalation.\n• Rybelsus must be taken correctly (empty stomach, plain water only, wait ≥30 min before other oral intake) or efficacy is significantly reduced.\n• Not a substitute for insulin in insulin-dependent diabetes (e.g., not for use in Type 1 DM or diabetic ketoacidosis).\n• Consider pausing before elective surgery with anesthesia due to delayed gastric emptying/aspiration risk (many societies now recommend holding dose day of/week before procedures - follow institutional protocol).\n• Significant weight loss efficacy makes it popular for off-label cosmetic weight loss; supply shortages have occurred due to high demand.\n• Reassess need for continued therapy periodically; weight regain is common after discontinuation.\n• Educate patients on signs of pancreatitis, gallbladder disease, and thyroid tumors.\n• Combine with lifestyle modification (diet, exercise) for optimal outcomes - not a monotherapy replacement for lifestyle changes.\n• SELECT trial (Wegovy) demonstrated 20% reduction in MACE in overweight/obese patients with established CVD without diabetes, expanding cardiovascular indication.\n• Higher-dose formulations (e.g., 7 mg/mL and 25 mg/3mL Wegovy vials) introduced to address supply and improve access.	t	2026-08-12 20:43:29.296432+05	2026-08-12 20:43:29.296432+05
04669937-995d-4cca-bb05-b375007c1d23	Loperamide	Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs)	Gastrointestinal System	Imodium	Opioid agonist in the GI tract; slows motility.	Diarrhea (acute/chronic).	4 mg initially, then 2 mg after each loose stool (max 16 mg/day).\n\nDose adjustments: None.	C. diff, ulcerative colitis, bloody diarrhea.	Constipation, abdominal cramps, dizziness.	P-gp inhibitors (quinidine, ritonavir).	Safe.	Hydration status.	Loperamide is poorly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract, with oral bioavailability of only about 0.3%, and the small fraction that is absorbed undergoes extensive first pass hepatic metabolism, mainly via CYP3A4 and CYP2C8 to N-demethylated metabolites, which limits systemic exposure further. It is also a substrate of intestinal and blood brain barrier P-glycoprotein, which actively pumps the drug back into the gut lumen and excludes it from the central nervous system at normal doses, explaining why it acts almost exclusively on peripheral opioid receptors in the gut wall. Plasma protein binding is high, around 95%, mostly to albumin. The elimination half-life ranges from about 9 to 14 hours, allowing twice to four times daily dosing. Elimination is primarily fecal as unchanged drug and metabolites, with a smaller renal component. Hepatic impairment can reduce first pass metabolism and increase systemic and CNS exposure, raising the risk of toxicity, so caution and dose reduction are advised in significant liver disease. Renal impairment has minimal effect on pharmacokinetics since renal clearance is a minor pathway.	Loperamide is a peripheral mu opioid receptor agonist that slows gut motility without central opioid effects at recommended doses. Warn patients never to exceed labeled doses, since supratherapeutic use (a known method of self-treating opioid withdrawal or seeking euphoria) saturates P-glycoprotein and CYP3A4/2C8, allowing CNS penetration and causing life-threatening QT prolongation, torsades de pointes, and cardiac arrest, sometimes called 'loperamide abuse cardiotoxicity.' Avoid in inflammatory bowel disease flares, C. difficile colitis, or bloody diarrhea due to risk of toxic megacolon. Concomitant CYP3A4/P-glycoprotein inhibitors (quinidine, ritonavir, itraconazole) markedly raise plasma and cardiac risk.	f	2026-08-12 20:31:45.18589+05	2026-08-17 18:51:49.761587+05
6c43cd3b-e595-405f-8e1c-b5796c90cb53	Empagliflozin	Sodium-glucose co-transporter 2 (SGLT2) inhibitor	Endocrine System / Cardiovascular System / Renal System	USA: Jardiance\nUK: Jardiance\nPakistan: Jardiance, Empafix, Empaglo\nIndia: Empagliflozin (various generics), Jardiance, Empa\nCombination products: Synjardy (with metformin), Glyxambi (with linagliptin), Trijardy XR (with metformin and linagliptin)	Empagliflozin selectively and reversibly inhibits sodium-glucose co-transporter 2 (SGLT2) in the proximal renal tubule, reducing renal glucose reabsorption and increasing urinary glucose excretion, thereby lowering plasma glucose independent of insulin secretion or action. This osmotic diuresis and natriuresis also contribute to reductions in blood pressure and intravascular volume, which along with other proposed mechanisms (reduced preload/afterload, improved cardiac energetics, reduced myocardial fibrosis) underlie its cardiovascular and renal protective effects.	1) Type 2 diabetes mellitus - as adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control (monotherapy or combination therapy)\n2) Reduction of risk of cardiovascular death in adults with type 2 diabetes and established cardiovascular disease\n3) Heart failure (HFrEF and HFpEF) - to reduce risk of cardiovascular death and hospitalization for heart failure in adults, regardless of diabetes status\n4) Chronic kidney disease - to reduce risk of sustained decline in eGFR, end-stage kidney disease, cardiovascular death, and hospitalization for heart failure in adults at risk of CKD progression	ADULTS:\nType 2 Diabetes: Initial 10 mg orally once daily in the morning, with or without food. May increase to 25 mg once daily if additional glycemic control needed and tolerated (eGFR >45 mL/min/1.73m2).\n\nHeart Failure: 10 mg orally once daily.\n\nChronic Kidney Disease: 10 mg orally once daily.\n\nRenal impairment dosing: \n- eGFR ≥30 mL/min/1.73m2: no dose adjustment typically needed for HF/CKD indications (10 mg)\n- eGFR <20 mL/min/1.73m2: not recommended to initiate (per FDA labeling for T2DM); may continue in HF/CKD per updated guidance down to eGFR 20 mL/min/1.73m2 with individualized assessment\n- Not for use in dialysis\n\nPEDIATRIC: Safety and efficacy not established in patients <18 years of age.\n\nNo dose adjustment required for hepatic impairment (use with caution in severe hepatic impairment).	- History of serious hypersensitivity reaction to empagliflozin or any component of the formulation\n- Severe renal impairment, end-stage renal disease, or dialysis (for initiation of T2DM indication)\n- Type 1 diabetes mellitus (not indicated; risk of diabetic ketoacidosis)\n- History of diabetic ketoacidosis on prior SGLT2 inhibitor therapy (relative)	COMMON: Urinary tract infections, genital mycotic infections (vulvovaginal candidiasis, balanitis), increased urination, thirst, nasopharyngitis, nausea.\n\nSERIOUS/BOXED WARNING:\n- Lower limb amputation (boxed warning, though risk primarily noted with canagliflozin; monitor foot health)\n\nOTHER SERIOUS:\n- Diabetic ketoacidosis (may occur with normal or near-normal glucose levels - euglycemic DKA)\n- Acute kidney injury and impairment in renal function\n- Volume depletion/hypotension, especially in elderly, renally impaired, or those on diuretics/antihypertensives\n- Necrotizing fasciitis of the perineum (Fournier's gangrene) - rare but life-threatening\n- Urosepsis and pyelonephritis\n- Hypoglycemia (when combined with insulin or sulfonylureas)\n- Increased LDL cholesterol\n- Bone fracture risk (mixed data)\n- Hypersensitivity reactions (angioedema, urticaria)	- Diuretics: Additive volume depletion, hypotension risk\n- Insulin/Insulin secretagogues (sulfonylureas): Increased hypoglycemia risk; may require dose reduction of secretagogue\n- Rifampin and other UGT inducers: May decrease empagliflozin concentrations\n- Lithium: SGLT2 inhibitors may decrease lithium concentrations\n- Antihypertensives: Additive blood pressure lowering effect\n- Other diabetic medications: Combination increases risk of hypoglycemia\n- Positive interference with urine glucose tests	PREGNANCY: Not recommended during pregnancy, particularly in second and third trimesters, due to potential fetal renal effects based on animal data showing adverse renal effects on developing kidneys. Discontinue when pregnancy is detected; switch to insulin for glycemic control if needed. Category not assigned per FDA's newer labeling format, but manufacturer advises avoidance especially in 2nd/3rd trimester.\n\nLACTATION: Not recommended during breastfeeding due to unknown effects on nursing infant and theoretical risk based on renal maturation concerns; use alternative agents (e.g., insulin) during lactation.	- Renal function (eGFR, serum creatinine) at baseline and periodically\n- HbA1c every 3 months until stable, then periodically\n- Blood glucose (self-monitoring as appropriate)\n- Blood pressure and volume status, especially in elderly or those on diuretics\n- Signs/symptoms of ketoacidosis (even with normal glucose) - especially during acute illness, surgery, prolonged fasting, or reduced caloric intake\n- Signs of urinary tract or genital infections\n- Lipid panel (LDL cholesterol)\n- Signs of Fournier's gangrene (perineal pain, tenderness, erythema, swelling with fever/malaise)\n- Foot examination for signs of infection, ulcers, or complications\n- Volume status and electrolytes, particularly in elderly, renally impaired, or on concurrent diuretics	ABSORPTION: Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentration (Tmax) reached in approximately 1.5 hours; absolute bioavailability ~78%; food does not significantly affect pharmacokinetics.\n\nDISTRIBUTION: Volume of distribution ~73.8 L; plasma protein binding ~86.2%.\n\nMETABOLISM: Primarily via glucuronidation by UGT2B7, UGT1A3, UGT1A8, and UGT1A9 enzymes; minimal CYP-mediated metabolism.\n\nELIMINATION: Half-life approximately 12.4 hours, supporting once-daily dosing. Eliminated via feces (~41.2%, primarily as unchanged drug) and urine (~54.4%, about half as unchanged drug).\n\nSPECIAL POPULATIONS: Exposure increased in renal impairment (AUC increases progressively with declining eGFR); mild-to-moderate hepatic impairment does not significantly alter pharmacokinetics.	- Empagliflozin has demonstrated significant cardiovascular and renal benefits beyond glycemic control, evidenced in landmark trials: EMPA-REG OUTCOME (CV death reduction in T2DM with established CVD), EMPEROR-Reduced and EMPEROR-Preserved (heart failure across full ejection fraction spectrum), and EMPA-KIDNEY (CKD progression).\n- Consider holding therapy 3-4 days before scheduled surgery to reduce risk of euglycemic DKA.\n- Educate patients on genital hygiene and signs of genital mycotic infections due to increased glucosuria.\n- Weight loss (modest, 2-3 kg) is an ancillary benefit due to caloric loss via glucosuria.\n- Does not cause significant hypoglycemia as monotherapy due to insulin-independent mechanism; caution advised when combined with insulin/sulfonylureas.\n- Preferred agent in patients with T2DM plus ASCVD, heart failure, or CKD per ADA/EASD and ACC/AHA guidelines due to proven outcomes benefits.\n- Effectiveness on glycemic control diminishes as renal function declines (reduced filtration limits mechanism of action), though cardiorenal benefits are largely preserved even at lower eGFR.\n- Sick day rules: advise temporary discontinuation during acute illness, significant dehydration, or before major surgery to reduce DKA risk.\n- Not for use in type 1 diabetes due to increased DKA risk without adequate insulin backdrop.	t	2026-08-12 20:59:19.531187+05	2026-08-12 20:59:19.531187+05
1f63ad9b-4d07-4a55-95df-1f26084a6754	Dapagliflozin	Sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) inhibitor (gliflozin class)	Endocrine System and Cardiovascular System	US: Farxiga\nUK and Europe: Forxiga\nPakistan: Forxiga, Dapa\nIndia: Dapa, Oxra, Dapagold, Dapakind\nCombination products (with metformin, saxagliptin, or sitagliptin) are marketed under separate brand names such as Xigduo, Qtern, and Qtrimet, depending on market.	Dapagliflozin selectively and reversibly inhibits sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 (SGLT2), the transporter responsible for the majority of glucose reabsorption in the early proximal renal tubule. By blocking SGLT2, dapagliflozin reduces renal reabsorption of filtered glucose and lowers the renal threshold for glucose, promoting urinary glucose excretion (glucosuria) and thereby lowering plasma glucose independent of insulin secretion or insulin action. This mechanism also produces mild osmotic diuresis and natriuresis, which contributes to reductions in blood pressure, plasma volume, and preload. Beyond glycemic effects, SGLT2 inhibition alters renal tubuloglomerular feedback, increasing delivery of sodium and chloride to the macula densa, which reduces afferent arteriolar vasodilation and glomerular hyperfiltration. This lowers intraglomerular pressure and albuminuria over time, explaining the drug's independent renoprotective effect. Cardioprotective and heart failure benefits are thought to arise from a combination of natriuresis, reduced preload and afterload, favorable shifts in myocardial energy substrate use toward ketone bodies, reduced epicardial adiposity, and possible direct effects on cardiac fibroblasts and sodium-hydrogen exchange, though the precise mechanism for the mortality and hospitalization benefit in heart failure is still being elucidated.	1. Type 2 diabetes mellitus: as an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control, alone or in combination with other antidiabetic agents including metformin, sulfonylureas, DPP4 inhibitors, and insulin.\n2. Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF) and heart failure with preserved or mildly reduced ejection fraction (HFpEF/HFmrEF): to reduce the risk of cardiovascular death and hospitalization for heart failure, regardless of diabetes status.\n3. Chronic kidney disease (CKD): to reduce the risk of sustained decline in eGFR, end-stage kidney disease, cardiovascular death, and hospitalization for heart failure in adults with CKD at risk of progression, regardless of diabetes status (evidence strongest for albuminuric CKD, per the DAPA-CKD trial).\nDapagliflozin is not approved for type 1 diabetes mellitus due to increased risk of diabetic ketoacidosis, and is not indicated for weight loss alone though weight reduction is a recognized secondary effect.	Type 2 diabetes mellitus (adults): Initial dose 5 mg orally once daily in the morning, with or without food. May increase to 10 mg once daily if additional glycemic control is needed and tolerated. Maximum dose is 10 mg once daily.\n\nHeart failure (HFrEF, HFmrEF, HFpEF), adults: 10 mg orally once daily, regardless of diabetes status.\n\nChronic kidney disease, adults: 10 mg orally once daily, regardless of diabetes status, continued as tolerated even as eGFR declines, down to an eGFR of approximately 25 mL/min/1.73m squared at initiation; therapy may be continued below this threshold if already established and tolerated since glycemic benefit diminishes but cardiorenal benefit is preserved.\n\nRenal dose adjustment: No dose adjustment is required based on renal function alone for the heart failure or CKD indications. For glycemic control, dapagliflozin has diminished glucose-lowering efficacy at eGFR below 45 mL/min/1.73m squared and is not recommended to be initiated for glycemic control if eGFR is below 25 mL/min/1.73m squared, although it may still be continued for its cardiorenal benefits at lower eGFR values as noted above under specialist guidance. Not recommended in patients with eGFR persistently below 25 mL/min/1.73m squared for initiation of any indication, and should not be initiated in dialysis patients.\n\nHepatic impairment: No dose adjustment required in mild to moderate hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh A or B). In severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh C), initiate at 5 mg once daily and monitor tolerability; if well tolerated, may increase to 10 mg once daily.\n\nPediatric dosing: Not established for children under 10 years of age. Limited approval or off-label use in some markets for type 2 diabetes in children 10 years and older at a dose of 10 mg once daily, but this is not universally approved and pediatric use should follow local regulatory guidance and specialist endocrinology input.\n\nElderly: No specific dose adjustment based on age alone, but elderly patients are at higher risk of volume depletion, hypotension, and should be monitored closely, particularly if also on diuretics.\n\nMissed dose: If a dose is missed, take as soon as remembered on the same day; do not double up the next day.	History of serious hypersensitivity reaction to dapagliflozin or any component of the formulation (including angioedema). Type 1 diabetes mellitus (increased risk of diabetic ketoacidosis, not an approved indication). History of diabetic ketoacidosis. Severe renal impairment with eGFR below approximately 25 mL/min/1.73m squared for initiation, and dialysis-dependent patients. Active or recurrent severe genital mycotic or urinary tract infections should prompt caution though not an absolute contraindication. Should not be used in patients with a history of bladder cancer without careful risk-benefit discussion, per some earlier labeling caution, though large-scale data have not confirmed an increased bladder cancer risk. Should be avoided in patients who are volume depleted or in those with acute severe illness, hypotension, or acute kidney injury until stabilized.	Very common to common: genital mycotic infections (vulvovaginal candidiasis in women, balanitis in men), urinary tract infections, increased urination, mild volume depletion symptoms including dizziness and postural hypotension, especially in elderly or those on diuretics or antihypertensives. Common: dyslipidemia (mild increase in LDL cholesterol), back pain, dysuria.\nSerious but rare: diabetic ketoacidosis, which may present with only modestly elevated or even near-normal blood glucose (euglycemic DKA), particularly in the setting of reduced caloric intake, surgery, acute illness, alcohol excess, or insulin dose reduction. Necrotizing fasciitis of the perineum (Fournier's gangrene), a rare but life-threatening soft tissue infection requiring urgent surgical referral. Acute kidney injury, usually related to volume depletion. Lower limb amputation risk was flagged in earlier canagliflozin trials and is mentioned as a class caution though the signal for dapagliflozin specifically is weaker. Hypoglycemia risk is low with dapagliflozin monotherapy but increases significantly when combined with insulin or sulfonylureas. Bone fracture risk was a signal in some early trials but has not been robustly confirmed for dapagliflozin. Hyperkalemia is uncommon and dapagliflozin's natriuretic effect generally makes hyperkalemia less likely than with other renoprotective agents.	Insulin and insulin secretagogues (sulfonylureas, meglitinides): additive glucose-lowering effect increases risk of hypoglycemia; dose reduction of the insulin or secretagogue is often needed when dapagliflozin is added.\nDiuretics, particularly loop diuretics: additive volume depletion and hypotension risk; monitor blood pressure and renal function closely, especially at initiation.\nACE inhibitors, ARBs, and other antihypertensives: additive blood pressure lowering and increased risk of acute kidney injury in the setting of volume depletion; generally a beneficial combination in CKD and heart failure but requires monitoring, particularly after initiation or dose changes.\nNSAIDs: additive risk of renal impairment, particularly in volume-depleted or elderly patients.\nLithium: SGLT2 inhibitor-induced volume changes may alter lithium renal clearance; monitor lithium levels if used concurrently.\nDrugs that increase risk of ketoacidosis in the setting of dapagliflozin: alcohol, ketogenic or very-low-carbohydrate diets, and prolonged fasting or surgery increase risk of euglycemic DKA and should prompt temporary discontinuation of dapagliflozin (typically 3 to 4 days before elective surgery).\nThere are no major clinically significant CYP450-mediated interactions since dapagliflozin is primarily metabolized via UGT1A9-mediated glucuronidation rather than oxidative CYP metabolism, though rifampin (a UGT and broad enzyme inducer) modestly decreases dapagliflozin exposure without requiring dose adjustment in most cases. Mefenamic acid, a UGT1A9 inhibitor, can modestly increase dapagliflozin exposure but is not considered clinically significant enough to require dose adjustment.	Pregnancy: Dapagliflozin is not recommended during pregnancy. Animal studies and the drug's mechanism of action, which affects renal maturation and fluid and electrolyte balance, raise concern for fetal and neonatal renal effects, particularly if used in the second and third trimesters when fetal kidney maturation is occurring. If pregnancy is detected or planned, dapagliflozin should be discontinued and glycemic control transitioned to insulin, which remains the preferred agent for diabetes management in pregnancy given the extensive safety experience. There is no adequate human pregnancy outcome data to support use.\nLactation: Dapagliflozin is not recommended during breastfeeding due to lack of human data and theoretical concern about effects on renal development in the nursing infant based on animal data showing SGLT2 expression in developing kidneys. Alternative antidiabetic therapy with a more established lactation safety profile, such as insulin or metformin, is preferred while breastfeeding.	Renal function (serum creatinine and estimated GFR): at baseline before starting therapy, then periodically, for example within a few weeks of initiation or dose change and at least annually thereafter, more frequently in CKD or heart failure patients or if concurrent diuretic or renin-angiotensin system inhibitor use.\nHbA1c: every 3 to 6 months when used for glycemic control, target individualized but generally less than 7% for most adults with type 2 diabetes, adjusted for frailty and hypoglycemia risk.\nBlood pressure and volume status: particularly in the first few weeks after initiation, and after any dose increases in diuretics or other antihypertensives, watch for symptomatic hypotension or dizziness.\nSigns and symptoms of genital and urinary tract infection: ask about at follow-up visits, counsel on hygiene.\nKetone levels (blood or urine) and clinical assessment for DKA symptoms such as nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, and fatigue, particularly during acute illness, reduced caloric intake, surgery, or when insulin doses are being reduced. Remember that glucose may be only mildly elevated or normal in euglycemic DKA associated with SGLT2 inhibitors.\nElectrolytes: periodically, particularly sodium and potassium, though clinically significant electrolyte disturbance is uncommon.\nLipid panel: monitor periodically given mild LDL cholesterol increase observed with SGLT2 inhibitors.\nWeight: track over time, as modest weight loss (typically 2 to 3 kg) is an expected secondary benefit.	Absorption: Rapidly absorbed after oral administration, with peak plasma concentration reached in approximately 1 to 2 hours. Absolute oral bioavailability is approximately 78%. Food has a modest effect on absorption but does not require dose adjustment; can be taken with or without food.\nDistribution: Approximately 91% protein bound, primarily to albumin. Volume of distribution is approximately 118 liters.\nMetabolism: Primarily metabolized via UGT1A9-mediated glucuronidation to an inactive metabolite, dapagliflozin 3-O-glucuronide. Minimal CYP450-mediated oxidative metabolism occurs, which accounts for the low potential for CYP-based drug interactions.\nElimination: Elimination half-life is approximately 12.9 hours, supporting once-daily dosing. Approximately 75% of a dose is excreted in urine (mostly as the inactive glucuronide metabolite, with less than 2% as unchanged drug), and approximately 21% is excreted in feces.\nSpecial populations: Exposure increases modestly with declining renal function, but this does not translate into a need for dose adjustment for the heart failure or CKD indications. Hepatic impairment (severe, Child-Pugh C) increases exposure moderately, warranting the lower starting dose noted above.	Counsel every patient started on dapagliflozin about genital hygiene and the classic early symptoms of vulvovaginal candidiasis or balanitis, since this is the adverse effect patients notice most and the one most likely to cause early discontinuation if not pre-empted; simple advice about hygiene and prompt treatment with topical antifungals usually allows continuation of therapy.\nThe euglycemic DKA warning is one of the most important practical points: do not be reassured by a normal or only mildly elevated glucose in a patient on an SGLT2 inhibitor who presents unwell with nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain. Check a blood ketone or venous blood gas early. This risk rises sharply around surgery, acute illness, and low carbohydrate intake, so it is standard practice to hold dapagliflozin for about 3 to 4 days before elective surgery and to counsel patients to withhold it during acute illness with poor oral intake, sometimes referred to as sick day rules.\nWhen starting dapagliflozin in a patient already on a loop diuretic, especially an elderly patient or one with borderline blood pressure, consider a modest reduction in diuretic dose at initiation to reduce the risk of symptomatic volume depletion and dizziness, then retitrate as needed.\nFor heart failure and CKD indications, remember that the benefit is essentially independent of baseline HbA1c or diabetes status. Non-diabetic patients started on dapagliflozin for heart failure or CKD do not need glucose monitoring for hypoglycemia but should still be counselled about genital infection risk and sick day rules.\nDapagliflozin causes a small, usually clinically insignificant, reversible dip in eGFR shortly after initiation, related to reduced intraglomerular pressure and hyperfiltration. This is an expected hemodynamic effect, not a sign of toxicity or a reason to stop the drug, provided the drop is modest, typically less than 30% from baseline, and stabilizes on repeat testing.\nWeight loss and modest blood pressure reduction are useful secondary benefits worth mentioning to patients as motivation for adherence, though the drug should not be used as a weight loss agent outside its approved indications.\nGlycemic efficacy diminishes as renal function declines, so in advanced CKD the primary rationale for continuing the drug shifts entirely to cardiorenal protection rather than glucose control, and this should be explained to patients so they understand why the medicine is still worthwhile even if their sugars are already well controlled on other agents.	t	2026-08-13 22:11:50.286609+05	2026-08-13 22:11:50.286609+05
649e95ac-bc5d-490c-a364-d36a0159f4b6	Fluconazole	Antifungals	Infectious Diseases	Diflucan	Inhibits fungal CYP450-dependent synthesis of ergosterol.	Candidiasis (vaginal, oral, esophageal), Cryptococcal meningitis.	150 mg single dose (vaginal); 100-400 mg daily.\n\nDose adjustments: Renal: reduce dose by 50% if CrCl <50.	Concomitant QT-prolonging drugs.	Headache, nausea, abdominal pain, rash.	Extensive (CYP3A4, 2C9, 2C19 inhibitor), warfarin, statins.	Avoid in pregnancy (high doses).	LFTs, renal function, K+.	Fluconazole is absorbed almost completely after oral dosing, with bioavailability exceeding 90% and unaffected by food or gastric pH, so oral and intravenous doses are considered interchangeable. Peak plasma concentrations occur roughly one to two hours after an oral dose. Protein binding is low, around 11 to 12%, and the drug distributes widely into body water, achieving concentrations in saliva, sputum, urine, vaginal secretions, and cerebrospinal fluid that approach 50 to 90% of simultaneous plasma levels, which underlies its efficacy in fungal meningitis. Unlike other azoles, fluconazole undergoes minimal hepatic metabolism, with less than 10% converted to inactive metabolites, so it is not a major substrate of extensive CYP450 turnover itself, though it is a potent inhibitor of CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 and a moderate inhibitor of CYP2C19. Elimination is predominantly renal, with about 80% of an administered dose excreted unchanged in the urine via glomerular filtration and tubular reabsorption. The elimination half-life in adults with normal renal function is long, approximately 20 to 30 hours, which supports once-daily dosing and allows a single dose to be effective for uncomplicated vaginal candidiasis. Half-life is prolonged significantly in renal impairment, roughly proportional to creatinine clearance, so dose or interval adjustment is required when creatinine clearance falls below 50 mL per minute. Fluconazole is dialyzable, and a supplemental dose after hemodialysis is recommended. Hepatic impairment does not require routine dose adjustment given minimal hepatic clearance, but caution and monitoring are advised because clearance mechanisms are not fully characterized in severe hepatic disease.	Fluconazole is a potent CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 inhibitor, so watch for interactions with warfarin, phenytoin, sulfonylureas, and statins, and it prolongs QT, so avoid combining with other QT-prolonging drugs. Renal dosing is essential, halve maintenance dose when creatinine clearance is under 50 mL per minute, and give a supplemental dose post-dialysis. It covers most Candida species well but has poor activity against Candida krusei and reduced activity against Candida glabrata, so consider susceptibility or switch to echinocandin if resistance suspected. Hepatotoxicity, though rare, warrants monitoring liver enzymes with prolonged courses. Avoid in pregnancy, especially high-dose regimens, due to teratogenicity risk.	f	2026-08-12 20:31:45.18589+05	2026-08-17 18:52:13.846962+05
8c42bfb9-d71d-453a-b216-3afdc9d86318	Digoxin	Anticoagulants & Antiplatelets	Cardiovascular System	Lanoxin	Inhibits Na+/K+ ATPase; positive inotrope, negative chronotrope.	Heart failure, Atrial fibrillation.	0.125-0.25 mg daily.\n\nDose adjustments: Renal: reduce dose based on CrCl.	Ventricular fibrillation.	Nausea, vomiting, yellow-green vision (halos), bradycardia.	Amiodarone, verapamil, diuretics (hypokalemia increases toxicity).	Caution.	Digoxin levels (0.5-2.0 ng/mL), HR, K+, renal function.	Digoxin is absorbed from the small intestine with oral bioavailability of about 60 to 80% for tablets and up to 90 to 100% for the liquid filled capsule formulation, and absorption is reduced by co administration with food that contains a lot of bran fiber or by malabsorption states. It is widely distributed into tissue, with a large apparent volume of distribution reflecting extensive binding to skeletal and cardiac muscle rather than plasma proteins, and only about 20 to 30% is bound to plasma proteins. Onset of action after oral dosing is about 30 minutes to 2 hours, with peak effect at 2 to 6 hours, and after intravenous dosing onset is 5 to 30 minutes with peak effect at 1 to 4 hours. Digoxin undergoes minimal hepatic metabolism, mainly via gut bacteria and to a small extent CYP3A4, and is largely eliminated unchanged by the kidneys through a combination of glomerular filtration and active tubular secretion via P glycoprotein. Elimination half life in patients with normal renal function is about 36 to 48 hours, but this is prolonged to 3.5 to 5 days or longer in anuric or severe renal impairment, so maintenance dosing intervals and loading strategies must be adjusted accordingly. Steady state is usually reached in about 7 days with normal renal function, longer with impairment, which is why loading doses are used when rapid effect is needed.	Narrow therapeutic index: target trough level 0.5 to 0.9 ng/mL for heart failure, toxicity risk rises above 2 ng/mL. Renal impairment, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, and hypercalcemia all increase toxicity risk. Major interactions: amiodarone, verapamil, quinidine, and clarithromycin raise levels via P glycoprotein inhibition, often requiring dose reduction by half. Toxicity presents with nausea, visual changes (yellow green halos), arrhythmias including bidirectional VT, and confusion in elderly patients. Always check renal function and electrolytes before initiating and periodically thereafter. Digoxin immune Fab is the antidote for severe toxicity or hyperkalemia from acute overdose.	f	2026-08-12 20:31:45.18589+05	2026-08-13 22:56:24.159245+05
c414c5c8-d89f-4b0f-9f6a-2b335f9a7603	Fluticasone	Bronchodilators & Inhaled Steroids	Respiratory System	Flovent (Inhaled), Flonase (Nasal)	Inhaled corticosteroid (ICS).	Asthma maintenance, Allergic rhinitis.	Inhaled: 44-220 mcg BID; Nasal: 1-2 sprays daily.\n\nDose adjustments: None usually required.	Status asthmaticus.	Oral candidiasis, dysphonia, epistaxis (nasal).	Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ritonavir).	Caution.	Growth in children, oral thrush.	Fluticasone propionate delivered by metered dose inhaler or dry powder inhaler has very low systemic bioavailability from the lung, generally under 1%, because most of the swallowed fraction undergoes extensive first pass hepatic metabolism, but the fraction absorbed through the respiratory epithelium reaches the systemic circulation directly and can still cause systemic effects at high doses. Oral bioavailability of swallowed drug is negligible, under 1%, due to poor absorption and near complete first pass extraction. Plasma protein binding is high, around 91%. It is metabolized almost entirely by the liver via CYP3A4 to an inactive carboxylic acid metabolite. Terminal half life after intravenous dosing is roughly 7 to 8 hours, though effective half life after inhalation is longer due to prolonged pulmonary retention and slow absorption. Elimination is mainly via feces as metabolites, with a small renal component, so dose adjustment for renal impairment is not usually necessary. Hepatic impairment reduces clearance and increases systemic exposure, so caution and lower doses are advised in significant liver disease. Fluticasone furoate, used in some newer inhalers and nasal sprays, has an even longer lung residence time and half life around 15 hours, allowing once daily dosing.	Counsel patients to rinse mouth after each dose to prevent oral thrush and dysphonia. Watch for systemic absorption at high doses, especially with potent CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ritonavir or itraconazole, which can precipitate iatrogenic Cushing syndrome and adrenal suppression, sometimes with severe adrenal crisis reported with ritonavir combinations. Not a rescue inhaler; must be used regularly with a spacer for MDI formulations to improve lung deposition and reduce oropharyngeal deposition. Long term high dose use raises risk of adrenal suppression, growth restriction in children, cataracts, and reduced bone mineral density, so use the lowest effective dose and monitor growth in pediatric patients.	f	2026-08-12 20:31:45.18589+05	2026-08-14 00:49:21.650455+05
7ddfa52b-9f95-48c3-a49b-8f32cfc2a08e	Cetirizine	Antihistamines	Respiratory System	Zyrtec	Second-generation H1 antagonist.	Allergic rhinitis, Urticaria.	5-10 mg daily.\n\nDose adjustments: Renal/Hepatic: 5 mg daily.	Hypersensitivity.	Drowsiness (mild), dry mouth.	CNS depressants.	Safe.	Sedation.	Cetirizine is a carboxylated metabolite of hydroxyzine and is rapidly and extensively absorbed after oral dosing, with peak plasma concentrations reached in about one hour. Food slightly delays the time to peak concentration but does not reduce overall absorption, so it can be taken with or without meals. It is highly protein bound, around 93%, and undergoes minimal hepatic metabolism, unlike many other antihistamines it is not significantly metabolized by the cytochrome P450 system, which explains its relatively low potential for drug interactions. The elimination half-life is roughly 8 hours in healthy adults. Excretion is predominantly renal, with about 70% of a dose recovered unchanged in urine and a smaller portion eliminated in feces. Because renal clearance dominates elimination, patients with reduced kidney function accumulate the drug significantly, and dose reduction is required in moderate to severe renal impairment, with avoidance or extended dosing intervals in end stage renal disease. Hepatic impairment alone has a more modest effect on clearance, but dose reduction is still advised in significant liver disease, and it should be used cautiously when both hepatic and renal impairment coexist. Levocetirizine, the active enantiomer, shares essentially the same pharmacokinetic profile at half the dose.	Cetirizine causes less sedation than first generation antihistamines but is not fully non-sedating, some patients report drowsiness, so caution with driving or operating machinery until tolerance is known. Reduce dose in renal impairment (creatinine clearance under 30 mL/min) and in hepatic impairment. Avoid combining with other CNS depressants or alcohol due to additive sedation. Considered compatible with pregnancy and breastfeeding based on accumulated clinical experience, though it does pass into breast milk in small amounts and can rarely cause infant drowsiness. Minimal CYP interaction potential is a practical advantage over older agents.	f	2026-08-12 20:31:45.18589+05	2026-08-14 00:51:08.349095+05
3f6e4289-ca98-4029-9c1d-7fafd527f3e3	Ibuprofen	Analgesics	Central Nervous System	Advil, Motrin	Reversible COX-1 and COX-2 inhibitor.	Pain, Inflammation, Fever.	200-800 mg every 6-8 hours.\n\nDose adjustments: Avoid in severe renal impairment.	Active peptic ulcer, severe HF, post-CABG.	GI upset, renal impairment, increased CV risk.	Aspirin, anticoagulants, ACEI/ARBs, lithium.	Avoid in 3rd trimester.	Renal function, BP, GI symptoms.	Ibuprofen is rapidly and almost completely absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract, with peak plasma concentrations reached in about 1 to 2 hours in the fasted state, though food delays but does not reduce the extent of absorption. It is highly protein bound, over 99 percent to albumin, which is clinically relevant in hypoalbuminemic states where free drug levels rise. The drug is extensively metabolized in the liver, mainly via CYP2C9 with a smaller contribution from CYP2C8, to inactive hydroxylated and carboxylated metabolites. Ibuprofen is a racemic mixture, and the inactive R enantiomer undergoes partial unidirectional conversion to the active S form. Elimination half life is short, roughly 2 to 4 hours, which is why regular dosing every 6 to 8 hours is needed for sustained anti inflammatory effect. Elimination is essentially complete within 24 hours, with about 90 percent of a dose excreted in urine as metabolites and conjugates, and less than 1 percent excreted unchanged; a small fraction is eliminated in feces via biliary excretion. Because renal excretion is of metabolites rather than active drug, dose adjustment for mild to moderate renal impairment is not usually required, but ibuprofen itself can worsen renal function and should be avoided or used cautiously with close monitoring in significant renal impairment. Hepatic impairment can reduce metabolic clearance and prolong half life, so lower doses and caution are advised in moderate to severe liver disease.	Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration, especially in patients over 65 or with cardiovascular risk, since NSAIDs increase risk of myocardial infarction, stroke, and heart failure even short term. Watch for GI bleeding risk, particularly with concurrent aspirin, corticosteroids, anticoagulants, or SSRIs. Ibuprofen can blunt aspirin's antiplatelet effect if taken before it, so counsel patients to take aspirin at least 30 to 60 minutes before ibuprofen or 8 hours after. Avoid in third trimester pregnancy due to risk of premature ductus arteriosus closure and oligohydramnios. Check renal function and blood pressure with chronic use, and avoid combining with ACE inhibitors and diuretics due to additive nephrotoxicity risk, the classic triple whammy.	f	2026-08-12 20:31:45.18589+05	2026-08-17 18:55:00.933737+05
12159b1a-20a7-41e8-8d00-9567c3586be1	Morphine	Analgesics	Central Nervous System	MS Contin, Kadian	Opioid agonist.	Severe pain, Dyspnea in palliative care.	15-30 mg every 4 hours (IR).\n\nDose adjustments: Renal: reduce dose or avoid (active metabolites).	Respiratory depression.	Constipation, sedation, hypotension, nausea.	CNS depressants.	Avoid.	Respiratory status, BP.	Morphine is well absorbed after oral, subcutaneous, intramuscular, intravenous, epidural, and intrathecal administration, but oral bioavailability is low and variable, roughly 20 to 40%, because of extensive first pass hepatic metabolism. Onset of analgesia is around 5 to 10 minutes after intravenous dosing, 15 to 30 minutes after intramuscular or subcutaneous injection, and 30 to 60 minutes after immediate release oral dosing, with peak effect around 60 minutes orally. Protein binding is modest, around 30 to 40%. The drug is metabolized primarily in the liver, and to a lesser extent in the intestinal wall and kidney, mainly by glucuronidation via UGT2B7 to morphine 3 glucuronide and morphine 6 glucuronide. Morphine 6 glucuronide is pharmacologically active and contributes significantly to analgesia and to toxicity, particularly in renal impairment, while morphine 3 glucuronide is inactive at opioid receptors and may contribute to neuroexcitatory effects such as myoclonus. Elimination half life is approximately 2 to 4 hours for the parent compound, though clinical effect can outlast plasma levels, especially with repeated dosing or extended release formulations, which have half lives that can appear longer due to prolonged absorption. Metabolites are renally excreted, so both elimination half life and risk of accumulation increase substantially in renal impairment, sometimes prolonging effective half life to well over 24 hours. Hepatic impairment reduces first pass metabolism, increasing oral bioavailability and systemic exposure, so dose reduction and longer dosing intervals are advised in significant hepatic dysfunction. Elderly patients generally have reduced clearance and increased sensitivity, warranting lower starting doses.	Start low and titrate slowly, especially in opioid naive, elderly, or renally impaired patients, since morphine 6 glucuronide accumulates in renal failure and can cause delayed, profound respiratory depression and sedation even after the parent drug has cleared. Avoid or reduce dose substantially in significant renal impairment; consider fentanyl or hydromorphone as safer alternatives. Watch for histamine mediated flushing, pruritus, and hypotension with rapid intravenous administration. Coadminister a stimulant laxative proactively, as constipation does not resolve with tolerance. Counsel patients on driving restrictions, avoidance of alcohol and sedatives, and signs of overdose. Naloxone should be available for at risk patients.	f	2026-08-12 20:31:45.18589+05	2026-08-17 18:57:10.492554+05
13f5fff9-9a8f-4b30-8c4e-6bdc45077089	Clotrimazole (Topical)	Topical Antibiotics & Antifungals	Dermatology	Lotrimin	Azole antifungal.	Tinea pedis, Tinea cruris, Tinea corporis.	Apply BID for 2-4 weeks.\n\nDose adjustments: None.	Hypersensitivity.	Erythema, stinging, blistering.	None.	Safe.	Skin irritation.	Clotrimazole applied topically to skin or vaginal mucosa penetrates the stratum corneum or vaginal epithelium and reaches fungicidal concentrations locally, with minimal systemic absorption. Less than 5 to 10 percent of a vaginal dose and even less of a cutaneous dose reaches systemic circulation. Absorbed drug is highly protein bound and undergoes hepatic oxidative metabolism via CYP450 enzymes to inactive metabolites, with elimination mainly in bile and feces and a small renal component. Because systemic exposure is negligible with normal application, a clinically meaningful systemic half-life is not established for topical or vaginal use, and there is no need for systemic pharmacokinetic based dose adjustment in renal or hepatic impairment when used as directed on intact skin or vaginal mucosa. Absorption increases somewhat with application to inflamed, denuded, or occluded skin, or under dressings, but remains low relative to oral azoles.	Effective for dermatophyte infections (tinea pedis, cruris, corporis), cutaneous candidiasis, and vulvovaginal candidiasis. Apply thin layer twice daily for two to four weeks depending on site; under treat relapse is common if stopped early. Local burning, itching, or irritation is common and usually mild; true allergic contact dermatitis occurs occasionally. Avoid contact with eyes. Vaginal cream and pessaries may weaken latex condoms and diaphragms. Systemic absorption is negligible so drug interactions and systemic adverse effects are not clinically relevant with correct topical use. Confirm diagnosis before treating, since unresponsive lesions often reflect misdiagnosis rather than resistance.	f	2026-08-12 20:31:45.18589+05	2026-08-17 20:23:55.267809+05
84e3f4fb-c536-4781-958f-990fdb2a0ccb	Losartan	Angiotensin II Receptor Blockers (ARBs)	Cardiovascular System	Cozaar	Blocks AT1 receptors, inhibiting Angiotensin II effects.	Hypertension, diabetic nephropathy.	25-100 mg daily.\n\nDose adjustments: Hepatic impairment: consider lower starting dose.	Pregnancy, concomitant aliskiren in diabetes.	Dizziness, hyperkalemia, back pain.	Potassium-sparing diuretics, lithium, NSAIDs.	Contraindicated.	BP, K+, renal function.	Losartan is an oral prodrug with only about 33% bioavailability because of extensive first pass hepatic metabolism. Roughly 14% of an oral dose is converted by CYP2C9 and, to a lesser extent, CYP3A4 into its active carboxylic acid metabolite, EXP3174, which is 10 to 40 times more potent at the AT1 receptor and accounts for most of the antihypertensive effect. Losartan itself has a short half-life of about 1.5 to 2 hours, while EXP3174 has a longer half-life of roughly 6 to 9 hours, supporting once daily dosing. Both parent drug and metabolite are highly protein bound, around 98 to 99%. Elimination is mainly hepatic and biliary, with about 60% excreted in feces and 35% in urine, only around 4% appearing unchanged in urine. No dose adjustment is generally required for renal impairment, though caution is needed in severe renal artery stenosis or volume depletion. In hepatic impairment, metabolite formation is reduced and starting doses should be lowered, since clinical experience in cirrhosis shows significantly higher plasma losartan concentrations.	Efficacy depends on CYP2C9 mediated activation, so CYP2C9 inhibitors like fluconazole can blunt the antihypertensive effect. Avoid combining with ACE inhibitors, aliskiren, potassium sparing diuretics, or potassium supplements due to hyperkalemia and renal injury risk. NSAIDs blunt efficacy and add nephrotoxicity, especially in volume depleted or elderly patients. Contraindicated in pregnancy, particularly second and third trimester, due to fetal renal toxicity and oligohydramnios, stop as soon as pregnancy is confirmed. Check renal function and potassium within one to two weeks of starting or up titrating. Useful uricosuric property makes it a reasonable ARB choice in hypertensive patients with gout.	f	2026-08-12 20:31:45.18589+05	2026-08-17 20:47:19.244087+05
